course: dictionary
lesson: 13014
title: Extravagant: too much money, too much show
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Extravagant is a word you will meet in money lessons, stories and adverts. It can mean spending much more money than is sensible or needed. «Sam spent all his birthday money on one extravagant toy he did not need.» The clue is the money: he spent far more than he should.
explain: Extravagant can also mean far too much, fancy or showy. «The king's extravagant palace had gold doors and forty towers.» Nobody needs forty towers to live in, so the clues show the palace is grander than it needs to be. Check it: swap in very fancy and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Extravagant claims go beyond what is true or reasonable. «The advert made extravagant claims that the trainers would make you run faster than a cheetah.» The word claims tells you it is about what someone says, and cheetah is a clue that the claim is too big to be true.
fact: Extravagant is an adjective, and the noun is extravagance.
fact: «Extravagant» means spending much more money than is sensible or needed.
fact: «Extravagant» can also mean far too much, fancy or showy.
fact: «Extravagant claims» go far beyond what is true or reasonable.
fact: Thrifty means careful with money, so it is the opposite of spending in an extravagant way.
exercise: «The couple had an extravagant lifestyle, buying new cars and expensive clothes every month.» What does extravagant mean here? | Saving every penny for the future | Quiet and simple | Sleeping late in the morning | *Spending much more money than is sensible
exercise: «The shop's extravagant gift wrap was covered in gold and sparkly bows.» What does extravagant mean? | *Fancy and showy, far more than needed | Plain brown paper | Too small to see
exercise: Find and copy one word that means showy and far too much. | quiet | tiny | dull | *extravagant
test: «Her extravagant party had a castle, fireworks and a band for twelve guests.» What does extravagant mean? | Small and quiet | *Far too much, fancy and showy | Sensible and cheap
test: «The advert's extravagant claim said the drink would make you grow two metres taller.» What does extravagant claim mean? | *A claim that goes far beyond what is true | A claim that is always honest | A claim written in a book | A claim about the weather
exam: «Mia saved her pocket money, while her brother's extravagant spending left him with nothing.» What does extravagant spending mean? | Counting coins | Saving every penny | Giving money to charity | *Spending much more than is sensible
